<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kimigayo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words c. 920; melody <a href="/years/1880">1880</a>; national anthem <a href="/years/1888">1888</a>; recognised in law <a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as 君が代</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The words of &quot;Kimigayo&quot; are an anonymous waka poem first found in the Kokin Wakashū, an anthology of about 920, which makes them the oldest words of any national anthem. The poem was copied into many later anthologies and sung by people of every rank as a wish for long life, at birthdays and other informal occasions, so no music was written down for it until the 19th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word kimi has been used for the Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> or for one's lord since at least the Heian period, and it can also mean &quot;my dear&quot;. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period the emperor was often called ohkimi, &quot;great lord&quot;, so it is disputed whether kimi in the title originally meant the emperor. The poem began with Waga Kimi wa, &quot;my lord&quot;, and the opening was changed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period while the rest stayed the same.</p><p>In 1869 John William Fenton, bandmaster of a British military band in Yokohama, suggested a national anthem to Iwao Ōyama, an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> domain, who chose the words and asked Fenton for a melody. The government set it aside because it lacked solemnity. In 1880 the Ministry of the Imperial Household adopted a melody by Yoshiisa Oku and Akimori Hayashi, based on a traditional mode of Japanese court music, and Franz Eckert added Western harmony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period the poem was a festive song among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> it became popular among the people. In the later Edo period it was a common New Year song in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ooku">Ōoku</a>, the women's quarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> domain. There kimi never meant the emperor: it meant the Tokugawa shōgun, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu clan</a> as rulers of Satsuma, a guest of honour, or all members of a drinking party. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> samurai from Satsuma controlled the imperial government and adopted the song as the national anthem.</p><p>By 1893 it was part of public school ceremonies. Around the turn of the 20th century the Christian leader Uchimura Kanzō argued that it was not the anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, because it praised the emperor, whereas an anthem should express the feelings of the people. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> schoolchildren in Japan and its colonies were ordered to sing it and salute <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Emperor Hirohito</a> every morning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After 1945 the song remained the de facto anthem. The Ministry of Education gave it no new meaning, which allowed it to be taken as meaning the Japanese people, but it did not formally renounce the prewar one. The Act on National Flag and Anthem passed the House of Representatives by 403 votes to 86 on 22 July 1999 and was enacted on 13 August. Prime Minister Keizō Obuchi said that kimi indicates the emperor as the symbol of the state and the unity of the people, and opposition parties objected. No official translation of the title or words has been set in law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kimigayo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the annexation of Korea by Japan in 1910, Japan's &quot;<b>Kimigayo</b>&quot; became the official anthem and the Korean one was banned.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aegukga">Aegukga</a></li><li><blockquote>The words of &quot;<b>Kimigayo</b>&quot; are an anonymous waka poem first found in the Kokin Wakashū, an anthology of about 920, which makes them the oldest words of any national anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a></li></ul></section>
